<div class="audit-log">
<%= render "audit_log/shared/nav" %>
<h1><%= AuditLog::Identity.for(@record_type, @record_id) %></h1>

<div class="tabs">
  <%= link_to "Changes", record_history_path(record_type: @record_type, record_id: @record_id, view: "changes"),
        class: ("on" if @view == "changes") %>
  <%= link_to "Actions", record_history_path(record_type: @record_type, record_id: @record_id, view: "actions"),
        class: ("on" if @view == "actions") %>
  <%= link_to "Timeline", record_history_path(record_type: @record_type, record_id: @record_id, view: "timeline"),
        class: ("on" if @view == "timeline") %>
</div>

<% if @view == "timeline" %>
  <p class="note">
    Both layers, interleaved, at the grain a person reads: one <strong>activity</strong> per
    <strong>unit of work</strong> rather than one per audit row, so a save that wrote this
    record and forty children is one activity and not forty. Anchored on
    <code>audit_changes</code>, so no write to this record can be missing — an action that
    named this record but wrote nothing to it lives on the <strong>Actions</strong> tab.
  </p>
  <p class="note small">
    This screen is built entirely from <code>AuditLog::Timeline</code>'s value objects — the
    same published contract a host app renders on its own pages. It is here so the contract
    cannot drift from what the auditor UI actually does.
  </p>
  <%# A narrowed timeline must never look like a complete one -- the rule the
      drill-down states its own window under. %>
  <p class="note small">
    Searched <strong><%= @timeline_scope.scope_description %></strong>.
    <% if @timeline_scope.bounded? %>
      <%= link_to "Search all retained history",
            record_history_path(record_type: @record_type, record_id: @record_id, view: "timeline") %>
      — slower, and touches every partition.
    <% else %>
      <%= safe_join([30, 90, 365].map { |d|
            link_to "last #{d} days",
              record_history_path(record_type: @record_type, record_id: @record_id,
                                  view: "timeline", days: d)
          }, " · ") %>
      — faster on a record with long history.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <%= render "audit_log/records/timeline_activities", activities: @activities %>
  <%= render "audit_log/shared/pagination", page: @page, rows: @page.records %>
<% elsif @view == "actions" %>
  <p class="note">
    The narrative layer for this record — the actions that named it as their
    <code>subject</code>. Readable, but complete only for actions that have a
    <code>Registry</code> entry <em>and</em> a <code>subject:</code> lambda, which is why
    <strong>Changes</strong> remains the compliance-grade answer. Unbounded in range, paged by
    keyset. Index: <code>(subject_type, subject_id, occurred_at DESC)</code>.
  </p>
  <%= render "audit_log/shared/event_rows", events: @events, changes_by_request: @changes_by_request %>
  <%= render "audit_log/shared/pagination", page: @page, rows: @events %>

  <%# The second population, kept visibly separate. See RecordTimeline: an action
      that merely touched this record is not the same claim as one that was about
      it, and this list is capped while the one above is not. %>
  <section>
    <h2>Also touched this record</h2>
    <p class="note">
      Actions that wrote to this record without naming it — a bulk update, a save whose
      subject was the parent, or an action registered with no <code>subject:</code> at all.
      Correlated through <code>request_id</code> against this record's own change rows, which
      is the only link there is between the two layers.
    </p>
    <%# A capped list must never be mistaken for a complete one -- the same rule
        the drill-down states its window under. %>
    <p class="note small">
      <% if @correlated.scanned.zero? %>
        No correlated change rows for this record.
      <% else %>
        Read from the <strong><%= @correlated.scanned %></strong> most recent
        <%= "change row".pluralize(@correlated.scanned) %> for this record.
        <% if @correlated.truncated? %>
          <strong>This record has more history than that.</strong>
          <%= link_to "Scan #{@correlated.scanned * 4} instead",
                record_history_path(record_type: @record_type, record_id: @record_id,
                                    view: "actions", scan: @correlated.scanned * 4) %>
          — slower. The <strong>Changes</strong> tab is not capped either way.
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </p>
    <% if @correlated.any? %>
      <%= render "audit_log/shared/event_rows", events: @correlated.events %>
    <% else %>
      <p class="empty">No unsubjected actions touched this record in the rows scanned.</p>
    <% end %>
  </section>
<% else %>
  <p class="note">
    The record layer, straight from the database triggers — full history for one record, and the
    only screen where an unbounded range is acceptable, because a single record has bounded
    history. Complete by construction: <code>update_all</code>, raw SQL and cascading deletes all
    land here. Paged by keyset, so there is no cap and no truncation.
    Index: <code>(record_type, record_id, occurred_at DESC)</code>.
  </p>
  <%= render "audit_log/shared/change_rows", changes: @changes %>
  <%= render "audit_log/shared/pagination", page: @page, rows: @changes %>
<% end %>

<p class="export"><%= audit_csv_link %> <span class="hint">the rows above, unpaginated, for the same filters and date range</span></p>
</div>
